WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.144
Remediation begins by updating controlled source inputs: the base image reference, operating-system packages, language runtime, lock files, application libraries, or code.

00:00:10.144 --> 00:00:19.836
Build from a reviewed definition rather than altering a running container, then assign the result a new immutable image digest.

00:00:19.836 --> 00:00:27.275
Automated tests should confirm that the image starts, passes health checks, and preserves application behavior.

00:00:27.275 --> 00:00:31.107
Unit and security tests catch local failures.

00:00:31.107 --> 00:00:36.292
Integration tests verify databases, messaging, identity, storage, and external services.

00:00:36.292 --> 00:00:41.025
A representative staging environment reveals deployment and platform interactions.

00:00:41.025 --> 00:00:44.406
Review changes beyond the named vulnerability.

00:00:44.406 --> 00:00:50.943
A newer base may update certificates, libraries, defaults, system users, or architecture support.

00:00:50.943 --> 00:00:55.226
Dependency upgrades may change APIs or data formats.

00:00:55.226 --> 00:00:59.960
Patch testing should reflect the application's actual operating paths.

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:08.300
Roll out through a controlled strategy such as rolling replacement, canary exposure, or blue-green deployment when appropriate.

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:15.739
Monitor errors, latency, resource use, health, and business indicators while the new image is introduced.

00:01:15.739 --> 00:01:20.923
Pause or reverse the rollout when defined limits are exceeded.

00:01:20.923 --> 00:01:27.460
Rollback planning identifies a known-good image and the conditions for returning to it.

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:38.506
It must also account for database migrations, message schemas, or external changes that may not be reversible merely by selecting an older container.

00:01:38.506 --> 00:01:45.494
Immutable infrastructure makes the deployed state traceable: rebuild, test, sign, promote, deploy, and verify.

00:01:45.494 --> 00:01:56.088
Emergency patches may compress timelines, but they should not abandon provenance, essential tests, monitoring, or follow-up work that restores the standard process.
